{"id":"repentance","name":"Repentance","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:repentance","torrey:repentance"],"groups":["The burden of the preaching","Unavailing","Instances Of","Israelites","The Jews of the exile"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Repentance","text":"There are three Greek words used in the New Testament to denote repentance. (1.) The verb metamelomai is used of a change of mind, such as to produce regret or even remorse on account of sin, but not necessarily a change of heart. This word is used with reference to the repentance of Judas (Matt. 27:3). (2.) Metanoeo, meaning to change one’s mind and purpose, as the result of after knowledge. This verb, with (3) the cognate noun metanoia, is used of true repentance, a change of mind and purpose and life, to which remission of sin is promised. Evangelical repentance consists of (1) a true sense of one’s own guilt and sinfulness; (2) an apprehension of God’s mercy in Christ; (3) an actual hatred of sin (Ps. 119:128; Job 42:5, 6; 2 Cor. 7:10) and turning from it to God; and (4) a persistent endeavour after a holy life in a walking with God in the way of his commandments. The true penitent …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H2398","lemma":"חָטָא","xlit":"châṭâʼ","gloss":"to miss","kjv":"bear the blame, cleanse, commit, by fault","count":43,"share":0.07},{"strong":"G3340","lemma":"μετανοέω","xlit":"metanoéō","gloss":"to think differently or afterwards","kjv":"repent","count":27,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H5162","lemma":"נָחַם","xlit":"nâcham","gloss":"to sigh","kjv":"comfort, ease, repent","count":29,"share":0.05},{"strong":"G3341","lemma":"μετάνοια","xlit":"metánoia","gloss":"compunction","kjv":"repentance","count":20,"share":0.03},{"strong":"H5771","lemma":"עָוֺן","xlit":"ʻâvôn","gloss":"perversity","kjv":"fault, iniquity, mischeif, punishment","count":27,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H2403","lemma":"חַטָּאָה","xlit":"chaṭṭâʼâh","gloss":"an offence , and its penalty, occasion, sacrifice,